 **What an app keeps.** Two JSON values. The tab's instance state, saved with the layout: `getState()` and `setState(value)` read and write one value, in the instance's state as `{ value }` (a root app's tab also keeps its folder, as above). `setState` rejects `too_large` above `apps.state_max_kb` (64) and rejects a value that is not JSON. Two tabs of one app keep separate values by construction, and a moved tab keeps its value. The app's store: one value for the app, shared by every tab of it in every window. `getStore()` answers the value, and `null` when the store file is absent, does not parse or is over `apps.store_max_kb` (256; a KB in this section is 1024 bytes); `setStore(value)` replaces it. A machine app's store is the file `~/.oculus/app-data/<name>.json` and a root app's is `<root>/.oculus/app-data/<name>.json`, created with its folder by the first write. It lies outside the app's folder, so writing it never changes the app's content hash or its approval. For a root app the store is a file of the root, tracked by git unless the root ignores `.oculus/app-data/`. `setStore` is a write of the new kind `app-store` (below): it rejects `too_large` above `apps.store_max_kb`, rejects a value that is not JSON, counts toward `apps.writes_per_min`, and rejects `refused:<reason>` when the file cannot be written, which is what a root that cannot be written gives. The server watches the store files, and a change to one, whoever made it (another tab, History's undo, a pull, a hand edit), reaches the page as a new `storeVersion` in the app's row on the `apps` channel (the page compares that alone), and the page then makes a fetch (below) and calls `onStore(value)` in every tab of the app in every window, the tab that wrote included. A write of a value equal to the one the file holds, as JSON values with object keys in any order, writes nothing, records nothing in History and answers success, so the file's bytes and `storeVersion` stay as they are whatever its formatting