ession overrides, and `deck`, which writes `config/present.yml`, an engine file (`server/edit/writes/config/deck.ts:1-4`). The other thirteen write routes (`calendar`, `connect`, `mail`, `memories`, `fs`, `open`, `source`, `deposit`, `git`, `hpc`, `usage/refresh`, `hook` and `terminal`) are closed to apps without exception: they run processes, write arbitrary files, reach a remote or act on an account, and the page never sends an app's request to any of them. `delete-entry`, `feed-delete`, `new-idea`, `linkedin-idea` and `linkedin-post` ask the reader first in a `Confirm` naming the app, the kind in words and the target as text, every time. Three of them are recorded with `undo: 'none'` and cannot be taken back, since `undoChange` answers `nothing` for such a record (`writes/undo.ts:188`): `new-idea` (`writes/manifest.ts:415`), `linkedin-idea` (`edit/feeds.ts:253`) and `linkedin-post` (`edit/feeds.ts:306`, which moves files). The other open kinds are recorded and undoable and send without asking. At most `apps.writes_per_min` (30) writes pass per frame per minute, `setStore` included, and the rest reject `rate`. The kind `app-store` is refused for a request that does not carry `x-oculus-app`, and a manifest that lists it in `writes` is refused `write_closed`, since the kind cannot be asked for. The body is `{ store: <value> }`, and the handler finds the store from the header's view id alone, writes nothing when the file already holds an equal value (above), and otherwise writes the file by rename, reads it back and refuses unless the read back equals the value sent. Its change record holds the file, the app's view id as `key`, the field `store`, and the sizes in bytes before and after as `before` and `after`. A store write's record is the one change record that holds sizes where every other holds the two values, and History draws the two sizes for it. The values before and after are kept beside the log in `~/.oculus/app-data/.undo/<time>.json`, named by the record's