+A document drawn as prose (a hub document, a project's document, this manual, the preview beside a Markdown file in the source tab, or a Markdown preview tab) gives every heading an anchor: `user-content-` and the heading's words as GitHub writes them. A paragraph holding only `[TOC]` is drawn as the document's table of contents: one link per heading that has words, in order, indented by level, as at the head of this page. A press on an entry, or on a link to `#` and a heading's anchor with or without its `user-content-` part (`#method` reaches the heading Method), scrolls the tab to that heading, and the page's address stays as it was.
 
 The LaTeX view and the project tab's LaTeX panel draw the same rows. A main file is a `.tex` file directly in the folder that declares a document class, so a folder that gains a supplement or a highlights file lists it the moment it is written; the project panel reads the `manuscript/` folder, and the LaTeX view reads any folder of a served root, opened from Open LaTeX in the Files menu on a folder, a root or a `.tex` file (for a file that builds through another main file, on that main file's folder). A `% !TEX root = <path>` comment names the main file a chapter builds through, and `% !TEX program = pdflatex`, `xelatex` or `lualatex` the engine `latexmk` builds a main file with; only the comment lines at the head of the file are read, in any case of `TEX`. Each row says when its PDF was last built, says `building` beside that time from the moment a build of the main file is asked for until its terminal has ended (a build a save started included), and offers Build and Clean, which run `latexmk` in the folder as terminals, so the output is in the terminal pane while it runs (Build on a main file that is already building starts no second `latexmk`: it shows the running build's terminal and builds once more after it); PDF, which opens the built PDF as a tab; Log, which opens the build's `.log` file as a source tab; Count Words, which runs `texcount` over the main file and the files it includes and shows the number until the file is built again; and Outline, the sections of the main file followed through its `\input`, `\include`, `\subfile` and `\import` commands, a press opening the file at the section's line; a file the parser refuses is listed by its sectioning commands alone, without the files it includes, and is named under the list as not fully read; a file over 2 MB, or one that cannot be read, is named under the list as not read, and a main file that was not read never says it holds no sections. The errors and warnings of the last build are listed under a count, errors first, as `file:line message` rows; a press opens the file at that line, and the same problems are drawn as markers in the editor of an open source tab. When a build ends the row redraws, and a PDF tab open on that file draws the new build without a reload. Saving a `.tex` file builds its main file in the background, without opening a terminal, unless `latex.auto_build` of `config/behavior.yml` is `never`; Save All in Open Editors and a Timeline restore save too and build the same way, and a main file that is already building is built once more after it, however many saves came. In the editor, resting the pointer on math or on an `\includegraphics` path shows the typeset formula or the picture, and Format Document (Shift+Alt+F) formats the file with the program `latex.formatter` of `config/behavior.yml` names, as LaTeX Workshop's formatting setting offers them: `none` (the default, which formats nothing and says so), `latexindent` (it comes with TeX Live and MacTeX), `tex-fmt` (`brew install tex-fmt`) or `badness`. Each program is found on PATH unless `latex.latexindent_path`, `latex.tex_fmt_path` or `latex.badness_path` names where it is; all five keys are in Settings. A program that is not found is named with where to set its path, and the formatted text is an unsaved edit that undo takes back.
 
 Every text file of the file browser opens in `source`. What counts as text is the file's own first bytes: no extension list decides it, so a script nobody gave an extension opens for editing and an archive somebody called a `.txt` is a download. Whether it can be saved is the file's mode on this machine: a file this machine locks, and one too long to have been read whole, are drawn as a read-only window under a line saying why, and neither takes a keystroke.
 
-A single click in the tree opens a preview tab, drawn in italics: the next single click in that pane is pointed at the file it names instead of opening a second tab, so a walk through a folder leaves one tab and not twenty. Clicking the same file a second time keeps its tab; so do a double click on the tab itself, typing into it, dragging it and splitting the pane around it. A double click on the row keeps it where the tree stays on screen beside the file, in a split pane; in one pane the first click has already put the file where the tree was, so the second click lands on the tab and keeps it the same way.
+A single click in the tree opens a preview tab, drawn in italics: the next single click in that pane is pointed at the file it names instead of opening a second tab, so a walk through a folder leaves one tab and not twenty. Clicking the same file a second time keeps its tab; so do a double click on the tab itself, typing into it, dragging it and splitting the pane around it. A double click on the row keeps it where the tree stays on screen beside the file, in a split pane; in one pane the first click has already put the file where the tree was, so the second press lands on the tab, in its editor or its frame, and keeps the tab the first click opened. The second press is taken for 500 ms after the first click, selects no word in the editor, and leaves the keyboard in the kept tab's editor or frame. A file the server says is gone opens in the source tab, which says it cannot be opened and offers Read again.
 
 The source tab's editor takes its options from `behavior.yml`: `editor.tab_size` and `editor.insert_spaces` for a file with no indented line, `editor.word_wrap` (`off`, `on`, `wordWrapColumn` or `bounded`), `editor.minimap`, `editor.render_whitespace` and `editor.sticky_scroll`, each a control in the Settings view's Editor group. `editor.languages` holds a record per language name with `tab_size`, `insert_spaces`, `word_wrap` and `rulers` (the columns that draw a vertical line), and a repository's `.oculus/settings.yml` can hold a `languages:` map of the same records, which overrides `editor.languages` for the files served from that root; its `editor:` key is not read, and an entry that cannot be read is skipped and listed under the Languages row. A value is taken in this order. Indentation: the choice made on the status bar for the tab, then the file's own when it indents with spaces or tabs, then the root's record, then `editor.languages`, then `editor.tab_size` and `editor.insert_spaces`; a file that indents with tabs keeps its tabs and takes its width from the same order. Word wrap: the tab's Alt+Z, then the root's record, then `editor.languages`, then `editor.word_wrap`. Alt+Z (View: Toggle Word Wrap, listed among the editor's palette actions) turns the wrap off when it is on, returns it to the configured value when it is off and the configured value wraps, and sets it on when both are off; the tab keeps the result across a reload. Rulers: the root's record, then `editor.languages`, then none. The minimap, whitespace and sticky scroll are the global keys alone, and the diff editor takes the configured word wrap, rulers, whitespace and sticky scroll of the file's language with no minimap. Sticky scroll pins the headings of a Markdown or LaTeX file, the blocks TypeScript, JavaScript, JSON, CSS, SCSS, Less and HTML give, and the braces of C, C++, Java, Dart, R and Rite. Every other editor setting stays at Monaco's default.
 
